{{- /*
  jira/md2jira — the reverse direction of jira2md: Markdown -> JIRA wiki markup,
  used when composing a description for a **new** ticket on a JIRA Server/Data
  Center instance (whose `description` field is wiki markup, not Markdown).
  Extracted for mitto-w8jp.3 ("JIRA: push to new"), which is the first caller.

  JIRA Cloud's v3 API generally renders Markdown-ish plain text acceptably, so
  this conversion is only needed for Server/Data Center. Callers should detect
  the instance (e.g. from `jira_get_server_info_jira` / `jira me` deploymentType,
  or simply attempt conversion when unsure) and skip straight to using the bead
  body unchanged when the target is Cloud.

  This fragment takes no arguments — usage:

      {{ template "jira/shared/md2jira" . }}
*/ -}}
Only convert when the target JIRA instance is **Server or Data Center** (wiki-markup
`description` field). For **Cloud**, skip this converter entirely and pass the
Markdown body through unchanged — Cloud's create-issue API renders Markdown-ish
plain text acceptably. When the instance type is uncertain, attempt conversion
anyway; a best-effort wiki-markup rendering degrades gracefully on Cloud too (worst
case, a few literal `h1.`/`{code}` markers appear in the description).

Write this **good-enough** Markdown → JIRA wiki markup converter once to a temp
file and reuse it — it preserves the most important structure (headers and code
blocks) and is not meant to be perfect:

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
# md2jira.py — good-enough Markdown -> JIRA wiki markup (reads stdin, writes stdout)
import re, sys

def convert(s):
    if not s:
        return ""
    fence = chr(96) * 3
    mono_open = chr(123) * 2
    mono_close = chr(125) * 2
    s = s.replace("\r\n", "\n")
    # Fenced code blocks ```lang ... ``` -> {code:lang} ... {code}
    def code_repl(m):
        lang = (m.group(1) or "").strip()
        body = m.group(2)
        tag = "{code:" + lang + "}" if lang else "{code}"
        return "\n" + tag + "\n" + body + "{code}\n"
    s = re.sub(r"(?s)" + fence + r"([A-Za-z0-9+#]*)\n(.*?)" + fence, code_repl, s)
    # Headers: #..###### -> h1. .. h6.
    s = re.sub(r"(?m)^(#{1,6})\s+(.*)$", lambda m: "h" + str(len(m.group(1))) + ". " + m.group(2), s)
    # Block quotes: > text -> bq. text
    s = re.sub(r"(?m)^>\s?(.*)$", r"bq. \1", s)
    # Links [text](url) -> [text|url]
    s = re.sub(r"\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)]+)\)", r"[\1|\2]", s)
    # Inline code `text` -> {{ "{{" }}{{ "}}" }} monospace (JIRA wiki markup)
    s = re.sub(r"(?<!`)`([^`\n]+)`(?!`)", lambda m: mono_open + m.group(1) + mono_close, s)
    # Bold **text** -> *text*
    s = re.sub(r"\*\*([^*\n]+)\*\*", r"*\1*", s)
    # Italic *text* (single, not already consumed by bold above) -> _text_
    s = re.sub(r"(?<!\*)\*([^*\n]+)\*(?!\*)", r"_\1_", s)
    return s

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.stdout.write(convert(sys.stdin.read()))
```

Use it by piping the composed Markdown description through it, e.g.
`python3 /tmp/md2jira.py < /tmp/desc.md > /tmp/desc.jira`. If `python3` is
unavailable, fall back to the Markdown body unchanged — never block ticket
creation on conversion.
